"""Private Gerion 2001 evidence reader. No Site mutations or publication."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,hashlib,re
B=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];O=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
SID='gerion2001';P='gerion-2001-'
read=lambda p:json.loads(p.read_text(encoding='utf8'))
sha=lambda p:hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest()
source=read(B/'source-manifest.json');manifest=read(B/'reader-assets/crop-manifest.json')
sections=[{'id':k,'title':v,'items':[]} for k,v in [('precursors','Precursors'),('protocol','Synthesis protocol'),('structures','Final structures'),('properties','Properties'),('intuition','Chemical intuition'),('sources','Sources and limitations')]]
items={}
SCOPE={'source_cohort':'Source-reported cohort only. Color names are not unique physical batch identifiers; Table 1 optical and Table 2 size cohorts are not silently joined.','method_context':'Preparation or acquisition context, not a new product or a demonstrated specimen-specific outcome.','model_context':'Author interpretation or proposed mechanism, not a directly measured atomic structure or optimized recipe.','cited_context':'Context cited by this article; cited full texts have not been independently read in this paper review.','source_metadata':'Bibliographic identity, coverage or source provenance; not experimental evidence.','author_outlook':'Proposed future work or design rationale, not an executed recipe.','curator_interpretation':'Explicit curation limitation or source conflict, separate from a source observation.'}
def ev(page,loc):return {'source_id':SID,'document_role':'main','pdf_page':page,'printed_page':8860+page,'locator':f'Main PDF p. {page}, printed p. {8860+page}, {loc}'}
def item(sec,key,title,text,page,loc,records=(),scope='source_cohort',kind='reported_source_fact',notes=()):
 assert key not in items,key
 e=ev(page,loc);d={'id':key,'title':title,'text':text,'claim_type':kind,'sample_scope':{'formulations':[],'physical_batch_id':None,'scope_kind':scope,'state':title,'link_limit':SCOPE[scope]},'evidence':[e],'source_locators':[e['locator']],'canonical_links':[{'record_id':P+r,'json_pointer':'','relation':SCOPE[scope]} for r in records],'notes':list(notes),'facts':[],'training_eligible':False}
 items[key]=d;next(s for s in sections if s['id']==sec)['items'].append(d);return d
def more(key,page,loc):
 e=ev(page,loc);items[key]['evidence'].append(e);items[key]['source_locators'].append(e['locator'])
def rows(sec,data):
 for row in data: item(sec,*row)

rows('precursors',[
('core-shell-stock','CdSe/ZnS starting dispersion','The starting CdSe/ZnS nanocrystals were synthesized in TOPO following references 1 and 11–13. This article does not reproduce their core-growth or ZnS-shell recipes. Stock is stored at room temperature in butanol/TOPO, approximately 1:2 v/v, at 5–10 mg CdSe cores per mL of solution. The mass basis is CdSe cores, not the total coated-particle mass. The stock remains stable for months without oxidation precautions; aged stocks were used in this work.',2,'Experimental, synthesis of CdSe/ZnS starting material',['core-shell-stock'],'method_context'),
('chemical-mps','MPS: thiol anchor and silica precursor','Mercaptopropyltris(methyloxy)silane, MPS, Sigma-Aldrich #175617, supplies a thiol and three hydrolyzable methoxy groups. The typical route initially uses 50 µL and later adds 20 µL. Figure 1 depicts the hydrolyzed Si–OH form for convenience, rather than the purchased Si–OCH₃ connectivity.',2,'Chemicals and Materials; silica-coating method',['silica-silanization'],'method_context'),
('chemical-aps','APS: optional amine functionality','Aminopropyltris(methyloxy)silane, APS, Sigma-Aldrich #281778, is the optional amine-functional silane. It is unsuccessful as the initial primer but can be added after MPS priming with the phosphonate silane. No APS dose is supplied; this is a partial functionalization variant.',2,'Chemicals and Materials',['aps-functionalization'],'method_context'),
('chemical-base','Two TMAH formulations','The source distinguishes tetramethylammonium hydroxide in methanol (#334901) from solid TMAH pentahydrate (#223212), both Sigma-Aldrich. Methanolic TMAH is added as 5 µL and 750 µL in priming and dilution; approximately 3 g pentahydrate basifies the quench mixture. A methanolic TMAH concentration is not given. Discussion describes its water content as less than 5%.',2,'Chemicals and Materials; silica-coating method',['silica-silanization'],'method_context'),
('chemical-phosphonate','Phosphonate silane: colloidal charge','The reagent is (trihydroxysilyl)propyl methylphosphonate in water, 42 wt/wt%, Sigma-Aldrich #435716. The typical method adds 600 µL during secondary shell growth. Its negative charge is intended to stabilize the aqueous particles; this does not establish a measured number of phosphonate groups on each nanocrystal.',2,'Chemicals and Materials; silica-coating method',['silica-silanization'],'method_context'),
('chemical-quench','Chlorotrimethylsilane: silanol quench','Chlorotrimethylsilane, Sigma-Aldrich #386529, is used at 2 mL in 20 mL methanol with approximately 3 g TMAH pentahydrate. It caps remaining silanol functionality; the authors describe hydrophobic methyl termination as the reason charged phosphonate groups must also be present.',2,'Chemicals and Materials; quench step',['silica-silanization'],'method_context'),
('chemical-mpa','MPA: alternative aqueous ligand','Mercaptopropionic acid, MPA, Sigma-Aldrich #M5801, is specified as 3-mercaptopropionic acid in the method. The alternative coating route adds 0.1 mL to 1 mL DMF. This thin-ligand control is chemically distinct from the crosslinked siloxane coating.',2,'Chemicals and Materials; MPA-coated nanocrystals',['mpa-exchange'],'method_context'),
('chemical-dmap','DMAP solution','4-(Dimethylamino)pyridine, DMAP, Sigma-Aldrich #D5640, is dissolved in DMF at approximately 20 mg/mL. Approximately 3–7 mL of this solution is added after MPA exchange. The solution becomes cloudy before recovery of the precipitate.',2,'Chemicals and Materials; MPA-coated nanocrystals',['mpa-exchange'],'method_context'),
('chemical-solvents','Solvents and process atmosphere','Anhydrous methanol precipitates the stock and is the silica-growth medium; DMF is the MPA-exchange medium. Ultrapure water is described as 18 MΩ Millipore water. Butanol stores the starting stock with TOPO; its isomer is not specified. Toluene and chloroform occur in optical or TEM dispersion contexts. Nitrogen is used for silanization and precipitate drying; no gas flow or pressure is stated.',2,'Experimental synthesis and material inventory',['silica-silanization','mpa-exchange','core-shell-stock'],'method_context'),
('chemical-topo','TOPO stock ligand','Trioctylphosphine oxide, TOPO, Sigma-Aldrich #346187, is the original hydrophobic coordinating ligand and stock component. Replacing it with MPS or MPA enables subsequent aqueous transfer. This source gives no new TOPO preparation or purification procedure.',2,'Chemicals and Materials',['core-shell-stock'],'method_context'),
('buffers','Buffers and ionic strength','The buffers are phosphate buffer (PB: K₂HPO₄/KH₂PO₄, Sigma #P8709/#P8584), MES [2-(N-morpholino)ethanesulfonic acid, #M5287] and Tris–borate–EDTA (0.5× TBE, #T3913). Calibrated NaCl addition varies ionic strength. Concentrations and pH are assay-specific; Figure 2 says PBS while the principal optical method says PB.',2,'Chemicals and Materials',['buffer-exchange','gel-electrophoresis'],'method_context'),
('analytical-reagents','Analytical reagents and substrates','DTNB, 5,5′-dithiobis(2-nitrobenzoic acid), is the Ellman thiol reagent from Sigma-Aldrich. Rhodamine 6G is the fluorescence standard and bleaching comparator. Glycerin is used as a 30 wt% stock for gel loading. Freshly cleaved mica supports AFM; ultrathin carbon films support TEM, and 400-mesh carbon-coated grids support EELS.',3,'Optical, TEM, EELS and AFM methods',['optical-acquisition','ellman-assay','tem-acquisition','eels-acquisition','afm-acquisition','gel-electrophoresis'],'method_context'),
('purification-materials','Purification media and devices','The source lists MWCO 10,000 dialysis tubing (#D9652), Sephadex G25 medium (#G2580), nylon Acrodisc filters (#PN4428T, Pall Gelman), YM-100 Centricon/Centriplus devices (Millipore), NAP columns (#17-0852-01, Amersham Pharmacia) and Slide-A-Lyzer cassettes (#66810, Pierce). The methods use MWCO 100,000 centrifugal filters. Materials-list 0.45 mm and protocol 0.45 µm filter units conflict and remain flagged.',2,'Chemicals and Materials',['silica-silanization','buffer-exchange'],'method_context'),
])
more('core-shell-stock',9,'Discussion: aged CdSe/ZnS stocks');more('chemical-mps',3,'Figure 1 caption');more('chemical-aps',9,'Discussion: APS control and later addition');more('chemical-base',8,'Discussion: water in TMAH')

rows('protocol',[
('silica-prime','Precipitate and prime with MPS','Precipitate 1 mL starting CdSe/ZnS stock, OD approximately 2, with anhydrous methanol. Dissolve the wet precipitate in 50 µL MPS, vortex and add 5 µL methanolic TMAH until optically clear. Precipitation-solvent volume, initial separation conditions and the OD wavelength are not specified.',2,'Silica-Coated Nanocrystals, first steps',['silica-silanization'],'method_context'),
('silica-primary','Primary shell formation','Dilute with 120 mL anhydrous methanol basified to pH approximately 10 with 750 µL TMAH. Place under N₂ in a 500 mL three-neck flask. Stir mildly for 1 h, heat gently to approximately 60 °C for 30 min, then cool to room temperature. No stir speed, temperature ramp or pressure is supplied.',2,'Silica-Coated Nanocrystals, primary shell',['silica-silanization'],'method_context'),
('silica-secondary','Secondary shell growth and functionalization','Add 90 mL methanol, 10 mL 18 MΩ water, 600 µL aqueous phosphonate silane and 20 µL MPS. Stir approximately 2 h, heat to approximately 60 °C for less than 5 min and cool to approximately 30 °C. The phosphonate reagent stock is 42 wt%; the source does not state a final phosphonate molarity.',2,'Silica-Coated Nanocrystals, second addition',['silica-silanization'],'method_context'),
('silica-quench','Quench remaining silanols','Add a mixture of 20 mL methanol and 2 mL chlorotrimethylsilane basified with approximately 3 g solid TMAH pentahydrate. Stir approximately 2 h, heat to approximately 60 °C for approximately 30 min and then stir at room temperature under N₂ for 2–4 days.',2,'Silica-Coated Nanocrystals, quench and aging',['silica-silanization'],'method_context'),
('silica-dialysis','Concentration, equilibration and dialysis','Use a Re111 Büchi rotary evaporator to concentrate by a factor of 2–5, then leave for 24 h. Dialyze against methanol for 1 day in MWCO 10,000 tubing and filter through 0.45 µm nylon. The later discussion recommends less than fourfold evaporation; both statements are retained rather than silently unified.',2,'Silica-Coated Nanocrystals, first purification',['silica-silanization'],'method_context'),
('silica-concentrate','Remove free silane and concentrate','Use YM-100 Centriplus/Centricon 100 centrifugal filters, MWCO 100,000, to remove excess free silane and reduce the solution to about 2 mL. Leave the methanolic dispersion for at least 12 h before solvent exchange. Filter centrifugation speed and duration are not stated.',2,'Silica-Coated Nanocrystals, centrifugal filtration',['silica-silanization'],'method_context'),
('silica-exchange','Transfer the fluorescent fraction into buffer','Use a NAP column or a home-packed Sephadex G25 column, 20 cm long and 0.7 cm in diameter containing approximately 5 g medium, equilibrated with 10 mM PB at pH approximately 7. Collect only fluorescent eluate, approximately 3 mL. Leave for a few hours and filter through 0.22 µm acetate.',2,'Silica-Coated Nanocrystals, solvent exchange',['silica-silanization'],'method_context'),
('silica-optional','Optional final aqueous purification','Optionally dialyze against 18 MΩ water for 1–4 days using MWCO 10,000, filter again and concentrate to the desired concentration in an Eppendorf #5301 vacufuge at 60 °C. The optional filter is printed as 0.22 mm, conflicting with the preceding 0.22 µm step. No target concentration is attached to this optional evaporation step.',2,'Silica-Coated Nanocrystals, optional purification',['silica-silanization'],'method_context'),
('silica-finish','Final clarification and storage','Centrifuge at 20,000×g for 30 min, discard the precipitate and store the supernatant in air. Typical OD at the absorption feature is approximately 0.3–1, described as 3–10 µM using an assumed extinction coefficient of 10⁵ M⁻¹ cm⁻¹. The optical path used in this conversion is not specified here.',2,'Silica-Coated Nanocrystals, final recovery',['silica-silanization'],'method_context'),
('mpa-exchange','MPA ligand exchange','Precipitate 1 mL CdSe/ZnS stock, OD approximately 2, with anhydrous methanol. Resuspend the wet precipitate in 1 mL DMF plus 0.1 mL 3-MPA. Vortex and sonicate approximately 10–30 min until transparent; leave at room temperature for 1–4 days.',2,'Mercaptopropionic Acid-Coated Nanocrystals',['mpa-exchange'],'method_context'),
('mpa-recovery','MPA-coated particle recovery','Add approximately 3–7 mL DMAP in DMF, approximately 20 mg/mL. Centrifuge the cloudy mixture at 3,000×g for 1 h; discard the supernatant and dry the precipitate under N₂. Dissolve it in 0.1–1 mL 18 MΩ water, then centrifuge at 20,000×g for 30 min and retain only the supernatant. The two centrifugations retain opposite fractions.',2,'MPA-Coated Nanocrystals, recovery',['mpa-exchange'],'method_context'),
('mpa-optional','Optional MPA removal','When necessary, pass the aqueous MPA-coated dispersion through a NAP column equilibrated with 18 MΩ water to remove excess MPA. The source gives no mandatory pass count, quantitative MPA endpoint or recovery yield.',2,'MPA-Coated Nanocrystals, last sentence',['mpa-exchange'],'method_context'),
('buffer-exchange','General buffer exchange options','The study changes buffers by NAP columns, dialysis using MWCO 10,000, or direct addition of concentrated buffer. These are alternative procedures chosen for the experiment, not three mandatory consecutive operations.',3,'Buffer Exchange',['buffer-exchange'],'method_context'),
('aps-variant','Optional APS addition after MPS priming','APS may be incorporated at the later shell-growth stage with the phosphonate silane, after initial MPS priming. This introduces surface amines and slightly increases fluorescence in the authors’ account. Amounts, exact APS-specific conditions and numerical improvement are absent, so this partial variant is not counted as another fully specified recipe.',9,'Discussion, APS functionalization',['aps-functionalization'],'method_context'),
('optical-acquisition','Absorption, emission and quantum yield','Absorption uses a Hewlett-Packard 8453 diode-array spectrometer and 2 mm quartz cuvette. Emission uses a Spex 1681 0.22 m/0.34 m spectrometer. Room-temperature quantum yields compare integrated emission of silica-coated particles in 10 mM PB with rhodamine 6G in water, assigned 95% yield from reference 24. Both solutions have OD 0.15 at 480 nm excitation.',3,'Optical Properties',['optical-acquisition'],'method_context'),
('storage-acquisition','Repeated-excitation storage assay','For storage stability, use 1 mL dispersion in 10 mM PB with OD 0.01 at the exciton feature and excite at 340 nm. Average the integrated fluorescence from four successive measurements and normalize to the first day. Figure 3 follows green, orange and red cohorts over a month.',3,'Optical Properties, photostability',['storage-photostability'],'method_context'),
('cw-acquisition','Continuous-excitation photostability assay','A thin quartz cuvette contains 1 µL solution, OD 0.065 at 488 nm. An Axiovert S100 TV epifluorescence microscope delivers 0.5 mW continuous-wave Ar⁺ light at 488 nm, with a stated 700 µm spot size. Record an approximately 10×10 µm region every 5 s for 4 h and normalize to initial intensity. The source does not define whether spot size means radius or diameter.',3,'Optical Properties, continuous illumination',['cw-photostability'],'method_context'),
('tem-acquisition','TEM acquisition and sizing','TEM uses a Topcon EM002B at 200 keV or JEM3010 ARP at 300 keV. Deposit approximately 5–10 µL dispersion in toluene, chloroform or water, OD approximately 0.3, on an ultrathin carbon-coated grid and dry in air. Record with a Gatan MSC794 CCD or digitize images; use standard image processing to measure diameters. Electron energy is kept in the source’s keV units.',3,'Transmission Electron Microscopy',['tem-acquisition'],'method_context'),
('eels-acquisition','EELS acquisition','Use a CM200 FEG TEM and approximately 10 µL sample on a 400-mesh carbon-coated grid, dried overnight. The Si and Se absorption edges are described at 110 and 67 eV, respectively. The spectra and spatial maps are not printed in the main article.',3,'Electron Energy Loss Spectroscopy',['eels-acquisition'],'method_context'),
('afm-acquisition','AFM acquisition and histogram construction','Place approximately 10 µL aqueous dispersion on freshly cleaved mica for 10 min, rinse with three drops of water and dry under N₂. Use a MultiMode AFM with TESP cantilevers in tapping mode in air. Heights are measured for more than 500 dots per sample. The methods say all features are counted, while Figure 5 and note 27 describe exclusion of low-height features from the displayed histograms.',3,'Atomic Force Microscopy',['afm-acquisition'],'method_context'),
('hplc-acquisition','Size-exclusion HPLC acquisition','Use a 30 cm G4000SWxl TSKgel column with 7 µm silica beads and 45 nm pores. Inject 20 µL sample in 10 mM PB, OD approximately 0.1. Elute with 10 mM PB plus 50 mM NaCl at pH approximately 7 and 0.5 mL/min; detect UV absorption at 210 nm. Retention alone is not a calibrated particle diameter.',3,'High-Performance Liquid Chromatography',['hplc-acquisition'],'method_context'),
('hplc-purification','Additional purification for the HPLC comparison','The red-trace green-emitting sample in Figure 6 receives additional MWCO 100,000 dialysis and repeated NAP 10 columns. This is distinct from the standard route’s MWCO 10,000 methanol dialysis. The two compared silanized preparations originate from the same green parent stock; they are not the same measured aliquot before and after treatment.',6,'Figure 6 and results discussion',['hplc-purification'],'method_context'),
('ellman-acquisition','Ellman thiol assay','Incubate nanocrystals with final DTNB concentration 1–10 mM in 50 mM PB at pH 7.3 for 2 h at room temperature. Compare absorption with and without DTNB. Nanocrystal concentration uses ε = 100,000 M⁻¹ cm⁻¹ at the first exciton; the thiol calculation uses the 412 nm absorbance difference and ε = 13,600 M⁻¹ cm⁻¹. The source attributes the latter coefficient to DTNB; its wording is retained.',4,'Ellman’s Test',['ellman-assay'],'method_context'),
('gel-acquisition','Gel electrophoresis','Use a Bio-Rad SubCell GT with 1–3% agarose in PB at pH approximately 7, MES at approximately 5.5 or 0.5× TBE at approximately 8.6. Mix 20 µL nanocrystals with 4 µL 30 wt% glycerin stock in the same buffer, vortex briefly and load. Typical runs use 50–100 V, corresponding to 3.3–6.7 V/cm, for 30–120 min. Image on a Stratagene 2020E white/UV table with EagleEye II CCD.',4,'Gel Electrophoresis',['gel-electrophoresis'],'method_context'),
('gel-ph','pH-comparison gel','Figure 7a compares green, yellow and red MPA-coated and silanized particles from the same parent samples, in separate lanes. Use 1% agarose for 1 h at 75 V, either 10 mM MES at pH 5.4 or 10 mM PB at pH 7.2, with no NaCl. The authors digitally enhanced the red-emitting lanes; the original processed figure is preserved.',7,'Figure 7a caption',['gel-ph-stability'],'method_context'),
('gel-salt','Salt-comparison gel','Incubate green MPA and silica-coated cohorts for 5 h in 30 mM PB at pH 7.3, with NaCl from 0 to 1,000 mM. Figure 7b labels 0, 1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50, 100, 200, 500 and 1,000 mM; the general methods omit the 2 mM level. Run in 1% agarose, 30 mM PB without NaCl, for 30 min at 100 V.',7,'Figure 7b and methods',['gel-salt-stability'],'method_context'),
('gel-narrowing','Electrophoretic band narrowing','Run green silanized particles in 1% agarose with 50 mM TBE for 1 h. The Figure 7c image says pH 8.5 and caption approximately 8.6. Extract a small band fraction by the filter-paper method cited in reference 26, then rerun beside an equal-concentration original sample. The complete extraction technique is not reproduced.',7,'Figure 7c caption',['gel-band-narrowing'],'method_context'),
])

rows('structures',[
('structure-scope','Core/shell/coating identity and structural limits','The reported architecture is CdSe core/ZnS shell with a silica-containing outer layer, or an alternative MPA ligand layer. The main article supplies TEM-derived core/core-shell diameters and AFM height distributions, but no source-specific atomic coordinates, unit-cell refinement, XRD or SAED pattern. A crystalline phase or downloadable measured CIF cannot be inferred from the architecture name alone.',6,'Table 2; full supplied main-article coverage',[],'curator_interpretation','reviewed_source_limitation'),
('tem-results','Core/shell size populations','The four core/shell populations are described as 4.2 (0.5), 4.4 (0.4), 5.6 (0.4) and 6.4 (0.4) nm for green, yellow, red and dark-red particles. The parenthetical widths are preserved without imposing an unstated statistical definition. Figure 5 shows the yellow TEM distribution; it is not a new raw micrograph.',5,'TEM results',['size-green','size-yellow','size-red','size-dark-red']),
('afm-results','Silica and MPA height distributions','AFM distributions are broader than the TEM core/shell distributions. Silanized samples show a roughly size-independent feature near 4 nm and a second feature near 6, 9, 14 or 17 nm; the MPA cohorts are near 5, 5, 9 or 12 nm. Heights above 20 nm are rare. These AFM heights are not crystallographic diameters or DLS hydrodynamic diameters.',5,'AFM results and continuation',['size-green','size-yellow','size-red','size-dark-red']),
('figure5-yellow','Yellow cohort: Figure 5 associations','The original Figure 5 compares yellow core/shell TEM diameters (top), silanized AFM heights (middle) and MPA-coated AFM heights (bottom) prepared from the same parent sample. Its horizontal label says diameter [nm], but the aqueous-coating measurements are heights. The plotted range is 0–20 nm and the vertical quantity is frequency; no bin-by-bin numerical table is supplied.',6,'Figure 5',['size-yellow']),
('afm-exclusions','Note 27: excluded low-height features','Every aqueous sample has a prominent 0.5–2 nm AFM feature that the authors attribute mainly to small silica complexes and residual ZnS particles. Note 27 says this feature is omitted and Figure 5 excludes heights below the corresponding core/shell diameter. These exclusions qualify the methods statement that all features were counted; they cannot be interpreted as proof of impurity-free samples.',11,'Note 27, spanning columns',['afm-acquisition','surface-interpretation']),
('shell-thickness','Shell thickness is an estimate','Table 2 defines shell thickness as half the difference between the coated AFM height and core/shell TEM diameter. The authors give approximate thicknesses of 1, 2, 4 and 5 nm and describe a maximum height increment below 11 nm. These combine different measurement modalities and broad distributions and should be treated as upper estimates, not atomically resolved uniform shells.',6,'Table 2 footnote and Discussion',['size-green','size-yellow','size-red','size-dark-red','surface-interpretation']),
('eels-result','Elemental evidence and its limitation','Preliminary EELS places Se predominantly in the core and Si in the surrounding layer, using the reported Se and Si edges. The authors state that the silica-layer result is not unambiguous and do not show the spectra. No EELS curve or map is reconstructed for this reader.',5,'TEM/EELS Results',['eels-acquisition','surface-interpretation']),
('si-microscopy','Declared HRTEM/AFM supporting information','The main text describes HRTEM evidence of an approximately 3 nm amorphous coating and explicitly points to supporting information. The SI declaration lists HRTEM and AFM figures of silanized nanocrystals. Until a local SI file is matched and inspected, these remain main-text descriptions of unavailable figures, not verified microscopy assets.',5,'TEM Results and SI reference',['surface-interpretation'],'source_cohort','reported_unshown_evidence'),
('hplc-three','Figure 6: three HPLC fractions','The blue trace shows a void feature near 13 min with 8% of total peak area, a fluorescent fraction near 15 min with 49% and a nonfluorescent fraction near 21 min with 43%. The last is tentatively assigned to siloxane complexes. Areas are detector-response fractions, not demonstrated mass fractions, particle-number fractions or chemical yields.',6,'Figure 6 and HPLC discussion',['hplc-acquisition']),
('hplc-purified','Figure 6: further-purified comparison','The red trace, after additional dialysis and NAP 10 purification, shows a dominant nanocrystal feature with substantially reduced small complexes and no appreciable nanocrystal signal in the void. Its inset gel gives almost the same migration as the blue preparation: 1% agarose, 10 mM PB, pH 7.2, 40 min, 50 V, 5 mm scale bar. Similar gel mobility does not show identical impurity content.',6,'Figure 6 and caption',['hplc-purification']),
('hplc-size-limit','What HPLC can and cannot establish','The authors infer that approximately 90–100% of the particles are smaller than the roughly 40–45 nm column-pore scale. Their comparison to about seven green nanocrystals does not establish monomers, dimers or a calibrated diameter distribution. Dimers, trimers and several cores in one silica shell cannot be ruled out.',9,'Discussion of particle sizes and HPLC',['surface-interpretation'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('upstream-zns','Note 28: possible residual ZnS particles','The starting core/shell dispersion has a sharp UV feature also seen when shell precursors are injected into hot pure TOPO without CdSe cores, and in shell-growth precursor stocks kept at room temperature for a few days. The authors use these comparisons to assign some small residual particles to ZnS. Exact precursor charges and temperatures are absent; this diagnostic comparison is not a complete new ZnS recipe.',11,'Note 28',['upstream-controls'],'source_cohort'),
])
for color,core,cs,sil,mpa in [('green',2.7,4.2,6,5),('yellow',3.1,4.4,9,5),('red',4.1,5.6,14,9),('dark-red',4.8,6.4,17,12)]:
 item('structures','table2-'+color,color.replace('-',' ').title()+' size cohort',f'Table 2 reports CdSe core TEM diameter {core} nm, CdSe/ZnS TEM diameter {cs} nm, silanized AFM height {sil} nm and MPA-coated AFM height {mpa} nm. These four states are linked within this table row. The same color word in Table 1 does not prove it is the identical physical batch.',6,'Table 2, '+color+' row',['size-'+color])
more('si-microscopy',10,'Supporting Information Available');more('afm-results',6,'Table 2 and AFM discussion')

rows('properties',[
('optical-overview','Absorption and fluorescence spectra','Figure 2 shows normalized absorption and emission for water-soluble silica-coated blue, green, yellow, orange and red nanocrystals. Blue has no recognizable absorption feature above approximately 450 nm and is absent from the absorption panel. The inset compares a green aqueous silanized sample with its parent core/shell dispersion in toluene. Visible absorption is largely retained and water-soluble spectra are shifted only about 1–2 nm to the red.',4,'Figure 2 and optical results',['optical-properties']),
('optical-width','Emission width and Stokes shift','The abstract reports emission FWHM 32–35 nm; Results describes about 32 nm. A roughly 20 nm Stokes shift is a broad summary, while Table 1 gives distinct absorption/emission differences. These are not five independently tabulated linewidths. Quantum yields depend on the starting core/shell batch.',4,'Optical results',['optical-properties']),
('storage-result','One-month fluorescence retention','Figure 3 presents normalized integrated fluorescence for green 544 nm, orange 595 nm and red 644 nm particles in 10 mM PB over approximately 30 days, with daily excitation. The prose estimates only about 1–5% loss per month. Four repeated measurements are averaged for each day; exact numerical time series are not supplied. These color cohorts are not automatically the Table 2 size cohorts.',5,'Figure 3 and optical results',['storage-photostability']),
('ph-optical','Short-term optical pH stability','The authors report unchanged quantum yield for one day at 6 < pH < 8. This observation is distinct from the gel-mobility pH range and does not establish long-term stability at arbitrary pH.',5,'Optical results',['optical-properties']),
('cw-result','Continuous-excitation fluorescence stability','Figure 4 compares silanized particles with rhodamine 6G. The nanocrystals initially brighten to approximately 120–200% of initial fluorescence before reaching a plateau; the source reports stable emission for at least about 4 h and dye bleaching after about 10 min. The displayed x-axis extends only to 4,000 s, so the 4 h statement is retained as prose, not an invented extension of the curves.',5,'Figure 4 and optical results',['cw-photostability']),
('thiol-result','Accessible surface thiols','Ellman analysis indicates several hundred thiol groups per nanocrystal, stable over roughly one month. The authors explicitly call this an upper limit: free thiol-containing siloxane complexes and uncertain nanocrystal concentration can inflate the result. No exact per-particle thiol count or uniquely resolved molecular surface is supplied.',7,'Surface Chemistry, Ellman results',['ellman-assay']),
('gel-charge','pH-dependent electrophoretic mobility','Silanized particles remain negatively charged across the tested pH 5.4–8.6 range and move toward the positive electrode. MPA-coated particles migrate more slowly at both pH 5.4 and 7.2; near pH 5.4 their motion is small, while the silanized bands move roughly half as far as at pH 7.2. The authors interpret these differences through surface charge rather than simply particle size.',7,'Figure 7a and results',['gel-ph-stability']),
('salt-result','Salt tolerance is batch-dependent','For the Figure 7b comparison, silanized-particle migration is relatively unchanged through 200 mM NaCl and slows above this level; MPA-coated particles are affected above about 20 mM. The authors separately report other silanized batches remaining unretarded after a week in 1 M NaCl. That stronger result is not assigned to every batch or to the plotted 5 h comparison.',7,'Figure 7b and salt-stability discussion',['gel-salt-stability']),
('narrowing-result','Band narrowing and recovery','The extracted fraction in Figure 7c produces a narrower electrophoretic band when rerun beside the original dispersion at equal concentration. Recovery is reported as up to 30%. This is electrophoretic fractionation of a distribution; it is not evidence of a new crystallite synthesis or a measured monodisperse size population.',7,'Figure 7c and band-narrowing discussion',['gel-band-narrowing']),
])
for color,a,e,q,r in [('blue',None,504,None,None),('green',522,544,18,81),('yellow',560,576,12,66),('orange',576,595,9,60),('red',620,644,5,71)]:
 txt=f'Table 1 reports emission at {e} nm. '+(f'Absorption maximum is {a} nm, quantum yield {q}% and retained yield relative to the TOPO-coated parent {r}%.' if a else 'No resolved absorption maximum is tabulated; the quantum yield and relative quantum yield are unreported because an absorption feature could not be assigned. Missing values are not zero.')
 item('properties','table1-'+color,color.title()+' optical cohort',txt,4,'Table 1, '+color+' row',['optical-properties'])

rows('intuition',[
('motivation','Biological labeling motivation','The authors motivate nanocrystals as tunable, bright, photostable labels with narrow emission and broad excitation, enabling multicolor observation using one excitation wavelength. Their comparison with organic dyes is cited background, not a blanket performance guarantee. Small hydrodynamic extent, colloidal stability and accessible coupling groups are the coating-design goals.',1,'Introduction and abstract',['literature-context'],'cited_context'),
('why-shell','Why a ZnS shell and a silica overlayer?','The ZnS shell is used to improve core photoluminescence through surface passivation; silica makes the originally hydrophobic stock dispersible in aqueous media and supplies coupling chemistry. The source frames silica as robust and biocompatible but does not conduct a toxicity or cellular-safety assay. Optical retention, size, charge and reactive groups are tested here.',1,'Introduction',['literature-context'],'cited_context'),
('prior-coatings','Earlier water-soluble coating strategies','References 14, 15 and 19–23 establish earlier biological labels, thiol ligand exchange and silica-encapsulation approaches. The present study compares its silica coating with MPA exchange on common parent stocks. It does not reproduce all earlier synthetic protocols or treat every cited system as a new data point.',2,'Introduction continuation',['literature-context'],'cited_context'),
('mps-binding','MPS anchoring and TOPO replacement','The authors propose deprotonation of MPS at pH 9–10 and thiolate binding to the Zn-rich shell, displacing TOPO. After adding a small base quantity, the dispersion becomes transparent and remains stable for hours. Excess base instead drives fast crosslinking and flocculation; no numerical excess-base threshold is supplied.',8,'Discussion, silanization mechanism',['silanization-mechanism'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('methanol-choice','Why methanol and limited water?','Methanol allows slower, more controllable exchange and shell formation than toluene. Toluene gives faster growth but poorer reproducibility on later aqueous transfer. The initially almost water-free basic methanol contains small water quantities from TMAH; the authors say hydrolysis proceeds above approximately pH 8.5. These explanations do not provide a quantified solvent-kinetic model.',8,'Discussion, solvent and pH control',['silanization-mechanism'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('primary-network','Hydrolysis and condensation','The proposed sequence hydrolyzes MPS methoxy groups into silanols, initially linked by hydrogen bonding, then uses mild heating near 60 °C to form Si–O–Si bridges with water release. Figure 1 is an explanatory mechanism, not measured atomic connectivity. The primary shell alone is not water-stable.',8,'Discussion and Figure 1',['silanization-mechanism'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('thermal-control','Heating and water-exchange failure controls','The source reports flocculation above about 70 °C or for heating much longer than 1 h. Removing unbound MPS by dialysis against basified methanol destabilizes the primary layer after about two days; water dialysis causes nearly immediate flocculation. These qualitative failures motivate secondary shell growth before aqueous exchange.',8,'Discussion, primary-layer stability',['synthesis-controls'],'source_cohort'),
('secondary-network','Secondary growth and charge installation','Fresh MPS and added water support further hydrolysis and crosslinking; the pH drops to about 9. Discussion calls the added water mixture approximately 10%, but the 90 mL methanol/10 mL water addition is not the entire reactor volume. Phosphonate-bearing silane installs negative charge, while optional late APS installs amines.',8,'Discussion, secondary shell',['silanization-mechanism'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('quench-rationale','Quenching limits continued aggregation','Without terminal quenching, silica networks continue growing and flocculate over days. Chlorotrimethylsilane blocks remaining silanols. Its methyl groups also make the surface more hydrophobic; attempts to remove phosphonate groups produced precipitation after a few days. The source therefore separates network termination from electrostatic aqueous stabilization.',8,'Discussion, quenching and charged groups',['silanization-mechanism','synthesis-controls'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('equilibration','Equilibration and purification tradeoffs','Concentrating too much creates cloudy dispersions and flocculation. The discussion recommends less than fourfold evaporation and adequate equilibration before later concentration. The authors empirically allow at least two days after quenching and roughly 12–24 h at later transitions; shortening the first interval produces partial precipitation. These recommendations supplement, but do not silently overwrite, the Methods sequence.',8,'Discussion, purification and equilibration',['synthesis-controls'],'source_cohort'),
('column-chemistry','Solvent exchange through Sephadex','The methanol plug perturbs roughly the top 5 mm of the Sephadex bed, but a sufficiently long column still separates the fluorescent particles. Cloudiness after elution is attributed to slow solvation and removed by 0.22 µm filtration. The authors estimate about a 50-fold reduction of free silica during purification, without a calibrated mass balance.',9,'Discussion, purification',['synthesis-controls','surface-interpretation'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('aps-control','Why APS is unsuitable as the first primer','APS initially dissolves the TOPO-coated stock but the first heating produces roughly tenfold stronger scattering and partial aggregation. The authors attribute this to weaker amine binding to the Zn shell compared with thiol anchoring. Late APS addition after MPS is tolerated and slightly increases fluorescence; this is not proof of a fully quantified optimized aminosilane route.',9,'Discussion, APS comparison',['aps-functionalization','synthesis-controls'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('note34-scattering','Note 34: scattering as a qualitative aggregation test','The authors use the fluorometer to detect light at 90° associated with the second harmonic of excitation. TOPO-coated CdSe/ZnS in methanol scatters almost 100 times more strongly than in toluene, which they say is not explained solely by refractive-index differences. MPS-primed particles in methanol show no such scattering. This is a qualitative observation, not a calibrated particle-size measurement.',11,'Note 34',['upstream-controls'],'source_cohort'),
('brightening-mechanism','Possible origin of illumination-induced brightening','Brightening is also seen for CdSe/ZnS in toluene, so the authors do not attribute it uniquely to silica. It is absent under vacuum, motivating an oxygen-related surface-passivation interpretation and a permeable silica layer. No oxygen uptake, pore-size distribution or molecular mechanism is directly measured.',9,'Discussion, luminescence behavior',['surface-interpretation'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('size-ambiguity','Microscopy does not uniquely identify every particle','AFM provides topography without chemical composition. Small features may be silica complexes or silica-coated residual ZnS; larger ones may be isolated coated particles, small aggregates or deposition artifacts. MPA adds less than about 1 nm for small green/yellow particles, whereas larger MPA heights are attributed to small aggregates. Irregular core/shell shapes and broad heights prevent an unambiguous monomer/dimer assignment.',9,'Discussion, AFM and shell thickness',['surface-interpretation'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('surface-chemistry','Surface groups and independent roles','The proposed outer layer contains thiol, phosphonate, methyl and residual silanol groups, with optional amines. Thiols supply conjugation handles and phosphonates provide charge. In the MPA comparison, carboxyl groups contribute both stability and coupling, so neutralizing them during coupling may weaken colloidal stability. The exact functional-group counts and atomic arrangement are unreported.',10,'Discussion, surface properties',['surface-interpretation'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('gel-interpretation','What broad gel bands imply','The strong pH dependence and the similar migration after removal of small HPLC components lead the authors to emphasize surface-charge heterogeneity. Broad bands cannot be read directly as broad crystal-size distributions. At acidic pH the MPA carboxyl surface is interpreted as nearly neutral, whereas silica-coated particles retain negative charge.',10,'Discussion, electrophoresis',['surface-interpretation'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('future-qy','Possible quantum-yield improvements','The authors suggest fresher starting CdSe/ZnS stocks or an additional shell such as CdSe/CdS/ZnS could improve aqueous quantum yields. This is an outlook, not an executed double-shell synthesis in this article.',9,'Discussion, optical quality',['literature-context'],'author_outlook','author_outlook'),
('future-bioconjugation','Bioconjugation and fractionation outlook','The paper notes covalent DNA attachment in recent work and discusses adapting band fractionation to select conjugation states, citing the gold/DNA context of reference 36. These ideas motivate future functionalization but provide no complete DNA-coupling protocol here. Low recovery and surface-charge heterogeneity remain practical limitations.',10,'Discussion and Conclusions',['literature-context'],'author_outlook','author_outlook'),
])

rows('sources',[
('identity','Source identity','Daniele Gerion, Fabien Pinaud, Shara C. Williams, Wolfgang J. Parak, Daniela Zanchet, Shimon Weiss and A. Paul Alivisatos, Synthesis and Properties of Biocompatible Water-Soluble Silica-Coated CdSe/ZnS Semiconductor Quantum Dots, Journal of Physical Chemistry B 2001, 105(37), 8861–8871, DOI 10.1021/jp0105488. Received February 12, 2001; final form April 17, 2001; published online June 5, 2001.',1,'Title, byline, dates and footer',[],'source_metadata','source_metadata'),
('coverage','Main article reviewed; declared SI not yet matched','All 11 supplied main pages have been read and visually inspected, including seven figures, two tables and all 36 references/notes. The local current and legacy main files have the same SHA-256 and count as one source. Supporting information is explicitly declared for HRTEM and AFM figures; no locally matched SI has yet been included in this proposal. Missing SI images remain a visible gap.',10,'Supporting Information Available',[],'source_metadata','reviewed_source_limitation'),
('cohort-boundaries','Sample and recipe boundaries','The two complete downstream methods are silica coating and MPA exchange of existing CdSe/ZnS stock. APS addition is incomplete as a quantitative recipe. Optical-color cohorts, Table 2 size cohorts, photostability experiments and gel/HPLC comparisons are linked only where the paper supports that association. No unique physical batch labels are invented, and cited upstream core/shell methods are not reconstructed.',2,'Methods, tables and figure associations',[],'curator_interpretation','curation_scope'),
('filter-conflicts','Conflicting filter units','The materials list prints a 0.45 mm nylon membrane while the purification procedure says 0.45 µm. The later optional filtration prints 0.22 mm while the preceding acetate filter is 0.22 µm. Both printed alternatives remain visible; a plausible correction is not silently treated as verified source data.',2,'Materials list and silica purification',[],'curator_interpretation','source_evidence_conflict'),
('optical-conflicts','Optical peak and buffer wording','The prose gives green/yellow emission as 542/572 nm, while Table 1 gives 544/576 nm. Table values define the optical table rows and the prose discrepancy is retained. Figure 2 caption says 10 mM PBS; the acquisition method and main discussion say PB. No unreported NaCl concentration is added.',4,'Optical Results, Figure 2 and Table 1',[],'curator_interpretation','source_evidence_conflict'),
('purification-conflicts','Concentration and waiting-time descriptions','Methods specify a 2–5-fold rotary concentration, while Discussion recommends less than fourfold. Methods place at least 12 h after centrifugal concentration before NAP exchange; Discussion describes roughly 12 h around an evaporation/centrifugation transition and emphasizes at least 24 h after evaporation. These statements are separate locators, not merged into one falsely exact timeline.',8,'Discussion compared with Methods p. 2',[],'curator_interpretation','source_evidence_conflict'),
('gel-conflicts','Gel panel labels and condition lists','Figure 7c labels pH 8.5 while its caption gives approximately 8.6. Figure 7b includes a 2 mM NaCl lane absent from the methods concentration list. Each displayed and textual value is preserved with its own locator; neither mismatch is used to fabricate an extra recipe.',7,'Figure 7 and methods p. 4',[],'curator_interpretation','source_evidence_conflict'),
('unreported','Fields that remain unreported','No exact upstream CdSe/ZnS growth recipe, main-coating yield, pressure, stirring speed, gas flow, temperature ramp, initial methanol precipitation volume, TMAH solution concentration, APS dose, raw spectrum arrays, full particle-size lists or source-specific atomic coordinates are supplied. These fields remain unknown. Biological compatibility is a design claim here, not a dedicated toxicology result.',2,'Full supplied main-article review',[],'curator_interpretation','reviewed_source_limitation'),
('affiliations','Affiliations and support','The authors are affiliated with UC Berkeley Chemistry and Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory’s Materials Sciences Division. Support includes the Swiss National Science Foundation, LLNL/NPSC, the German DFG, FAPESP 99/08603-7, NIH 1R01RR14891-01, DOE DE-AC03-76SF00098 and DARPA N00014-99-1-0728. The acknowledgments identify microscopy-facility and colleague assistance; they do not add experimental samples.',10,'Acknowledgments',[],'source_metadata','source_metadata'),
])
